while many fans couldn't really make  sense of jared leto's joker and suicide  squad there's no arguing against the  fact that the movie's clown prince of  crime had a really unique look  but now writer director david ayer has  confessed that he made a mistake with  the character's design  whether you love it or you hate it jared  leto's joker design makes an impression  from his pale white skin and bright  green hair to his eye catching tattoos  and silver teeth  he has a look that screams gangster  meets prankster  but was this stylized design really the  right approach  over two years after suicide squad's  theatrical run ayer has confessed that  he now considers one particular piece of  letto's joker design to be a total  mistake  the admission came about thanks to a fan  on twitter who asked ayer about the  joker's eye-catching forehead tattoo the  one that simply reads  damaged when asked what inspired that  particular bit of ink  ayer admitted that it was his idea and  he regrets it the director responded to  the query with a  maya culpa writing yes i have to fall on  that sword it was one step too far  even after owning up to what he now  feels was a bad move  ayer affirmed that he still generally  likes the tattoo at least in concept he  also gave some insight into his thought  process as to why the joker would have  such a statement scribbled across his  cranium  in a follow-up post ayer wrote in my  mind it was meant for batman after he  smashed  joker's teeth for killing jason todd  some people found it alienating  originality and faithfulness to the  canon are often at odds  batman and joker are my two favorite  characters ever  ayer offered a similar explanation for  the joker's damage tattoo around the  time suicide squad launched in theaters  saying that joker would have gotten the  tattoo while locked up in arkham asylum  after being scooped up by the dark  knight  as he told empire in august 2016 it's in  the asylum where joker would have done  the damage tattoo as a message to batman  saying  you've damaged me i was so beautiful  before and now you've destroyed my face  that's where the grill comes from as it  happens ayer has quite a few regrets  about the decisions he made with suicide  squad many of which come down to the  choices made about the joker  not only does ayer now wish he didn't  ink up the joker's forehead  he also regrets not making the character  the main bad guy of the movie's story  despite being heavily featured in the  movie's promotional materials  jared leto's joker only has about 10  total minutes of screen time in the  theatrical cut of suicide squad  far less than what was originally shot  for the super villain picture  even people who enjoyed the movie have  said that it could have been improved by  an increased focus on the evil jester  and some have approached ayer on twitter  with their thoughts in response to one  fan who wrote him a year after the  movie's release  ayers said believe me i agonize over  this  yes joker should have been the main bad  guy yeah  ayer has been expressing some  disappointment in the final cut of  suicide squad since shortly after the  film opened in theaters  in january 2017 the director penned a  lengthy post detailing what he wished  he'd done differently  then shared a screenshot of the note on  his twitter page in the post he wished  for a time machine to undo the errors he  feels he made  along with a determination to do better  next time as for what he'd do  differently he said  i'd make joker the main villain and  engineer a more grounded story i have to  take the good and bad and learn from it  i have to give the characters the  stories and plots they deserve next time  real talk as we know now he'd also do  away with that distracting tattoo  ayers self-criticism is refreshing but  he doesn't have to be so tormented  as divisive as suicide squad turned out  to be upon release the movie still  performed well enough for warner  brothers in dc to turn a massive profit  off of it  and despite the critical drubbing plenty  of fans still truly enjoyed the film  joker and all it can even be argued that  crafted the most  unforgettable version of the joker that  has yet to grace the screen  making a major impact on pop culture  despite the character's slight screen  time like the rest of suicide squad  ayers joker isn't necessarily a disaster  it's just  a little damaged
 

